\details{
The \code{robots_product_token} is used ONLY for robots user-agent group
selection; it is never sent as an HTTP header. Each matcher backend is
authoritative only for its own vendor semantics: \code{google} for Google,
\code{yandex} for Yandex, \code{bing} for Bing, and RFC 9309 behavior only under the
\code{rfc9309} backend. The Google backend additionally accepts any valid Google
robots product token and returns Google parsing/matching semantics for it.
The Yandex and Bing backends are bounded to their supported vendor profiles
only and never generalize to arbitrary tokens. This boundary is published
as \code{robots_engine_contract_v1()$matcher_capability}.

Since schema revision \code{2026-11-22.1}, a row on the \code{google} or \code{rfc9309}
backend whose token is not one or more ASCII letters, \verb{_} or \code{-} (the
\code{identifier} rule of RFC 9309 section 2.2.1, published as
\verb{matcher_capability$<backend>$product_token_grammar}) is refused under
every ruleset: \code{matcher_status = "not_evaluated"},
\code{reason = "input_invalid"}, \code{error_stage = "input"} and
\code{error_class = "robots_invalid_product_token"}, one row each, with no
error raised. Google's matcher cuts each \code{User-agent} value in the file at
its first other character and compares the cut value with the token as
given, so such a token (\code{Googlebot/2.1}, a full HTTP \code{User-Agent} string,
a leading or trailing space, \code{*}, digits, a letter outside ASCII) could
never name a group and would silently read \verb{User-agent: *}. RFC 9309's
\code{product-token} is \code{identifier / "*"}, but \code{*} names no crawler, so a
caller's token must be an \code{identifier}. The token is not trimmed or
rewritten: \code{error_message} names the leading valid part
(\code{Googlebot} for \code{Googlebot/2.1}) as a hint only. The \code{yandex} and \code{bing}
backends keep their own refusals, and \code{\link[=allowed_by_robots_text]{allowed_by_robots_text()}} and
\code{\link[=allowed_by_robots_url]{allowed_by_robots_url()}} read any token as before.

Under the \code{google} ruleset with the \code{google} backend, the result is also a
crawler-aware layer above the matcher for the Google crawler tokens
published as \code{matcher_capability$google$crawler_tokens} (schema revision
\code{2026-11-12.1}). A crawler that Google's common-crawlers page lists with a
parent token (\code{Googlebot-Image}, \code{Googlebot-Video}, \code{Googlebot-News},
\code{Google-InspectionTool} and \code{Google-CloudVertexBot} list \code{Googlebot};
\code{GoogleOther-Image} and \code{GoogleOther-Video} list \code{GoogleOther}) obeys the
groups naming its own token, else those naming its parent token, else
\code{*}; the two are never merged. \code{AdsBot-Google}, \code{AdsBot-Google-Mobile},
\code{Mediapartners-Google} and \code{APIs-Google} ignore \code{*}, so with no group of
their own they are allowed, with no line. The token is compared whole and
ASCII case-insensitively (\code{Googlebot-Image/1.0} is not listed). For these
tokens the result follows what Google documents for its crawlers; it is
read from Google's documentation, not observed from the crawlers. Under
every other ruleset, and for every other token, it is Google's matching
for the token, NOT a claim of compatibility with, or a prediction of, the
crawler the token names. The \code{group_token} column says whose group
decided a Google row: the token as given, its parent token, \code{"*"} when no
group names it (the \code{*} rules, if any), or \code{NA} for a \code{*}-ignoring
crawler with no group of its own, a row of another backend, or a row no
matcher decided. Under a \code{preset} it is the matcher token of the preset
as the contract spells it (\code{"OAI-SearchBot"}, or \code{"Claude-User"} for a
caller's \code{"claude-user"}), \code{"*"}, or \code{NA}; see below.
\code{\link[=allowed_by_robots_text]{allowed_by_robots_text()}} and \code{\link[=allowed_by_robots_url]{allowed_by_robots_url()}} apply none of
this layer.

The Yandex backend is active (since schema revision \code{2026-07-18.2}). An
evaluated Yandex row publishes \code{reason} as one of \code{default_allow},
\code{rule_allow}, \code{rule_disallow}, or \code{effective_empty_disallow}; a non-evaluated
Yandex row reports \code{matcher_status = "not_evaluated"} with
\code{reason = "unsupported_product_token"}
(\code{error_class = "robots_unsupported_product_token"}) or
\code{reason = "invalid_request_target"}
(\code{error_class = "robots_invalid_request_target"}) at
\code{error_stage = "input"}. The activation adds the public
\code{matched_rule_value_raw} list column (one element per row: exact owning-rule
bytes, \code{raw(0)} for an effective-empty Disallow, or \code{NULL} for an absent
rule); it is populated for Yandex-evaluated rows and \code{NULL} elsewhere. The
vendored-matcher identity is published as
\code{robots_engine_contract_v1()$matcher_identity$yandex}.

A Google row reports no line for a rule with an empty value: when the
selected rule is an \verb{Allow:} or \verb{Disallow:} with nothing after the colon,
