backslash, another character RFC 3986 excludes, or a \verb{\%} without two hex
digits) is still evaluated. A URL with no authority, such as \code{"/a/../b"},
keeps the bytes before its first \code{/} and has its dot segments removed from
there on, unless that would start the path with \verb{//} and so read as a
host (\code{"/a/..//h/x"}): then it is evaluated as given. Under \code{"none"} the
matcher reads the URL as given, as before. The \code{url} column always holds
the input and \code{matcher_url} the URL the matcher read.
\code{url_preprocessing_note} is \code{NA} under \code{"none"}, and under \code{"rfc3986"}
when the input is a valid RFC 3986 URI with an authority and the
preprocessing changed nothing. Otherwise it says why the input is not a
valid URI, that it has no authority, and which rule changed it (or why
its dot segments were left in place), joined by \code{"; "}.
\code{\link[=allowed_by_robots_text]{allowed_by_robots_text()}} and \code{\link[=allowed_by_robots_url]{allowed_by_robots_url()}} read the URL as
given.

Since schema revision \code{2026-11-21.2}, \code{"whatwg"} reads the URL as the
WHATWG URL Standard serializes it (through \code{rurl::serialize_url()}),
which models browsers and Node \code{fetch}. It removes literal and encoded
dot segments (\verb{/\%2e\%2e/} too) and, unlike \code{"rfc3986"}, changes the
encoding and host as that standard does (a space becomes \verb{\%20}, a byte
outside ASCII is percent-encoded, the host is put in lower case and a
default port dropped). Userinfo is left out and the fragment dropped, as
under \code{"rfc3986"}. The URL's bytes are read as UTF-8 whatever the
session's locale (a \code{"latin1"}-marked URL is converted from latin1). A
URL with no scheme, such as \code{"/a/../b"}, \code{"example.com/a/./b"} or
\code{"//h/a/../x"}, is split exactly as under \code{"rfc3986"}: only its path, when
that starts with \code{/}, is serialized (on the placeholder origin
\verb{http://h.invalid}, with its query), and the serialized path and query
follow the same lead \code{"rfc3986"} keeps (\code{"/b"}, \code{"example.com/a/b"},
\code{"//h/x"}). Any other scheme-less URL is read as under \code{"rfc3986"}, and
one whose serialized path would start with \verb{//} after a lead with no
authority (\code{"/a/..//h/x"}) is evaluated as given. A URL it cannot parse is
evaluated as given, with the note
\verb{not a valid WHATWG URL: evaluated as given}; a changed URL has the note
\verb{WHATWG URL serialization changed the URL}, then \verb{userinfo removed} or
\verb{fragment dropped} where those apply. \code{"whatwg"} serializes a URL of any
length, and its cost grows faster than linearly on a long path with many
dot segments (in \code{rurl::serialize_url()}).

Under \code{"rfc3986"} and \code{"whatwg"}, \code{preprocessing_disagreement} compares
the URL the matcher reads under \code{"rfc3986"} with the one it reads under
\code{"whatwg"}, on the rows the matcher decided (\code{url_decision} not \code{NA}).
Under \code{"rfc3986"} the \code{"whatwg"} form is computed only for those rows;
under \code{"whatwg"} it is the URL the matcher reads, so it is computed for
every non-empty row. Comparing with the WHATWG form makes the default
\code{"rfc3986"} cost about 3 to 10 times the old preprocessing; \code{"none"} is
unchanged. Under \code{"none"} the flag is never computed: a decided row is
\code{NA}, with \code{disagreement_clients}
\verb{not compared under url_normalization = "none"}, and nothing beyond the
old reading runs. It is \code{TRUE} when the two differ and
are decided differently, or when \code{"whatwg"} cannot parse the URL, and
\code{FALSE} otherwise. Any other row (an \code{NA} or empty URL, an empty token,
\code{capability_unavailable}, a policy decision, a token or target the
backend refuses) is always \code{FALSE}, with \code{NA} \code{disagreement_clients}.
The second match runs only on rows whose two URLs differ in more than
the scheme or host case, a default port, an empty path against \code{/}, or
the fragment. \code{disagreement_clients} is \code{NA} when the flag is \code{FALSE};
otherwise it gives each side's decision. When the two forms differ only
through encoded dot segments (\verb{\%2e}, \verb{.\%2e}, \verb{\%2e.} or \verb{\%2e\%2e} as a
whole segment, either case), it also names the clients surveyed to
handle dot segments that way:
\verb{dot segments as in: wget 1.25, curl/libcurl < 8.13.0} for \code{"rfc3986"}
and \verb{dot segments as in: Chrome 157, Node 26 fetch, curl/libcurl >= 8.13.0} for \code{"whatwg"}. Otherwise it says the forms differ in more than
dot segments and that no client survey covers this, or, when \code{"whatwg"}
cannot parse the URL, gives the \code{"rfc3986"} decision and says so. Under
\code{"rfc3986"}, a decided row
whose URL is longer than 2048 bytes is not compared: the \code{"whatwg"} form
is not computed, no second match runs, \code{preprocessing_disagreement} is
\code{NA} and \code{disagreement_clients} is
\verb{not compared: URL longer than 2048 bytes}. Under \code{"whatwg"} every length
is compared.

Since schema revision \code{2026-11-28.1}, \code{preset} reads the file as one
measured AI user agent does, on the API surface its complete probe
matrix ran on: agent-view's live probes of 2026-11-12 to 2026-11-26,
published with an evidence tier, a source and the dates per behavior as
\code{robots_engine_contract_v1()$agent_presets}.
A preset fixes the \code{"google"} ruleset and backend, the group the matcher
selects and the URL steps; \code{robots_product_token},
\code{robots_policy_ruleset} and \code{matcher_backend} may be left out, and one
given must agree with the preset (the token compared ASCII
case-insensitively), while \code{url_normalization} must be left out. A
conflicting argument raises an error of class
\code{robotstxtr_preset_conflict}. \code{"chatgpt_user"} is ChatGPT-User behind the
OpenAI Responses API \code{web_search} tool: it obeys only a group naming
\code{OAI-SearchBot} (\code{group_token} \code{"OAI-SearchBot"}), never a
\code{User-agent: ChatGPT-User} group or \code{*}, so a file with no
\code{OAI-SearchBot} group allows it, with \code{group_token} \code{NA}; it puts the
hex digits of every \verb{\%HH} escape in the URL in upper case, which is
inferred from one verdict rather than seen; and it keeps a \code{/} in the
query, which one case saw sent raw. \code{"claude_user"} is Claude-User
behind the Claude API \code{web_fetch} tool (\code{web_fetch_20260309}), not the
ClaudeBot crawler: it selects its own group, else \code{*}, and, as edge logs
show, puts \verb{\%HH} hex digits in upper case, encodes each \code{/} in the query as
\verb{\%2F}, and strips one trailing \code{/} from a path other than \code{/} when the
URL has no query or fragment, so \verb{Allow: /dir/index.html} with
\verb{Disallow: /} disallows \verb{/dir/} for Claude-User and allows it for
ChatGPT-User. Nothing is decoded, and a \verb{\%} without two hex digits is
left as it is. No other byte is changed: a raw space or byte outside
ASCII, a tab or other control byte, a backslash, a character RFC 3986
excludes (\verb{"<>^\{|\}} and the backquote), a \verb{\%} without two hex digits, a
literal or percent-encoded dot segment, a \verb{//} run, userinfo, and the
trailing-slash cases not listed are unmeasured, so they are read as
given and \code{url_preprocessing_note} names them
(\verb{left as given, unmeasured for <preset>: ...}, the inputs joined by
\code{", "}) after the steps that changed the URL, the parts joined by
\code{"; "}. A preset row is not compared for disagreement:
\code{preprocessing_disagreement} is \code{NA} with \code{disagreement_clients}
\verb{not compared under an agent preset}. Both presets read Google's
512,000-byte window, which is Google's: the ChatGPT cap is unmeasured, and
the Claude API refuses the whole host for a robots.txt over 500,000 bytes,
which the preset does not model yet. The agents' cached copies (ChatGPT
follows \code{OAI-SearchBot}'s stored copy; the Claude API reuses a recent
read and caches a refusal) are not modeled either: a preset reads the
body it is given. ChatGPT Chat and \code{claude.ai} have earlier observations
(the ChatGPT group binding was counted in Chat mode too, and the Claude
size refusal on \code{claude.ai}) but no complete probe matrix, so the
presets are scoped to the two APIs.
}
\examples{
# A non-Google-named token evaluated through the default Google backend
# yields Google parsing/matching semantics for that token. This is NOT a
# prediction of the named crawler's behavior.
decisions <- robots_evaluate_text_v1(
  "user-agent: *\ndisallow: /private",
  "https://example.com/private",
  robots_product_token = "Yandex",
  robots_policy_ruleset = "google",
  matcher_backend = "google"
)
decisions$results$matcher_backend  # "google" -- Google semantics, not Yandex
decisions$results$url_decision     # "disallow"

# The same file read as two measured AI user agents: Claude-User strips
# the trailing slash, so the index.html shortcut no longer allows /dir/.
body <- paste0(
  "user-agent: *\nuser-agent: OAI-SearchBot\n",
  "allow: /dir/index.html\ndisallow: /"
)
claude <- robots_evaluate_text_v1(
  body, "https://example.com/dir/", preset = "claude_user"
)
claude$results[c("url_decision", "matcher_url", "url_preprocessing_note")]
chatgpt <- robots_evaluate_text_v1(
  body, "https://example.com/dir/", preset = "chatgpt_user"
)
chatgpt$results[c("url_decision", "group_token")]
}
